{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. Name the side to move, describe the central structure and give a plan for your course side. Name the opponent's most relevant counterplay. No single move is required. ANSWER: White to move. White king g1; central-file pawns f2, c3, d3, e4. Black king g8; central-file pawns c5, d5, e6, f7. Compare the pawn contacts and opened files before choosing a move. Branch plan to reassess: Check whether e5 is safe, then consider Nf1 and h4 only if the center is stable. White's queen bishop needs a route, often through f4 or g5 after development. Black can play ...Qc7, ...b5 or ...dxe4. A kingside attack is premature if e4 can simply be captured. Check which threats remain possible in this exact diagram. } *

