{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. Name the side to move, describe the central structure and give a plan for your course side. Name the opponent's most relevant counterplay. No single move is required. ANSWER: White to move. White king g1; central-file pawns c2, f2, d3. Black king g8; central-file pawns e4, e6, c7, f7. Compare the pawn contacts and opened files before choosing a move. Branch plan to reassess: Use e4 with sufficient support and prepare Re1 if needed. The queen can sometimes unpin Nf3 by moving from d1, but move it only for a clear reason. Copying the Slav plan can be inaccurate because Black can play ...c5 in one move. White's d3 pawn can become a target after central exchanges. Check which threats remain possible in this exact diagram. } *

