{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. Name the side to move, describe the central structure and give a plan for your course side. Name the opponent's most relevant counterplay. No single move is required. ANSWER: White to move. White king g1; central-file pawns f2, c3, d3, e4. Black king g8; central-file pawns d4, e5, c7, f7. Compare the pawn contacts and opened files before choosing a move. Branch plan to reassess: Use a central lever and complete development. Consider exd5 when the resulting open file can be used by a rook rather than merely exchanging for its own sake. Black may play ...Nbc6 or ...c5, gaining more space. Do not waste a tempo preparing to displace a knight that is not on f6. Check which threats remain possible in this exact diagram. } *

